### What is `Collectors.counting` as a downstream collector?

`counting()` is a collector that counts the elements it receives and returns the total as a `Long`. Used as the downstream of `groupingBy`, it counts the elements in each group, producing a map from key to count. This is the canonical frequency-map idiom.

```java
package com.bank.text;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import static java.util.stream.Collectors.counting;
import static java.util.stream.Collectors.groupingBy;

public class CountingDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> words = List.of("SALARY", "RENT", "SALARY", "EMI", "SALARY", "RENT");
        Map<String, Long> freq = words.stream()
                .collect(groupingBy(w -> w, counting()));
        System.out.println(freq);
    }
}
```

```text
{EMI=1, RENT=2, SALARY=3}
```

### How do you build the frequency map from narration strings?

Transaction narrations are sentences. Split each narration into words, flatten them into one stream, normalize case, and group by word with `counting`.

```java
package com.bank.text;

import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import static java.util.stream.Collectors.counting;
import static java.util.stream.Collectors.groupingBy;

public class NarrationFrequency {
    public Map<String, Long> wordCounts(List<String> narrations) {
        return narrations.stream()
                .flatMap(n -> Arrays.stream(n.toUpperCase().split("\\s+")))
                .filter(w -> !w.isBlank())
                .collect(groupingBy(w -> w, counting()));
    }
}
```

```java
// Example input
List<String> narrations = List.of(
        "SALARY CREDIT NATIONAL BANK",
        "RENT PAYMENT",
        "SALARY CREDIT",
        "EMI PAYMENT NATIONAL BANK");
```

```text
{SALARY=2, CREDIT=2, NATIONAL=2, BANK=2, RENT=1, PAYMENT=2, EMI=1}
```

### Why can you not sort a map directly by value?

A `Map` is not ordered by value, and most map implementations are not ordered at all. `TreeMap` orders by key, not value. To rank by frequency you must take the map's entries into a stream and sort those entries.

```java
// WRONG expectation: a TreeMap does NOT sort by value
Map<String, Long> freq = new java.util.TreeMap<>(wordCounts);
// This orders by WORD alphabetically, not by COUNT
```

```java
// CORRECT: stream the entries and sort by value
List<Map.Entry<String, Long>> ranked = freq.entrySet().stream()
        .sorted(Map.Entry.comparingByValue(java.util.Comparator.reverseOrder()))
        .toList();
```

### How do you sort the entries by count descending and take the top N?

Stream `entrySet()`, sort with `Map.Entry.comparingByValue` wrapped in `Comparator.reverseOrder()` so the largest count comes first, then `limit` to the top N.

```java
package com.bank.text;

import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

public class TopWords {
    public List<Map.Entry<String, Long>> topN(Map<String, Long> freq, int topN) {
        return freq.entrySet().stream()
                .sorted(Map.Entry.<String, Long>comparingByValue(Comparator.reverseOrder()))
                .limit(topN)
                .toList();
    }
}
```

```text
// top 3 of the sample narrations
SALARY=2
CREDIT=2
NATIONAL=2
```

### How do you make ties deterministic?

When several words share the same count, their relative order is otherwise unspecified. Chain a secondary comparator on the key so ties break alphabetically, which makes the ranking stable across runs.

```java
// WRONG: ties among equal counts order unpredictably
freq.entrySet().stream()
        .sorted(Map.Entry.comparingByValue(Comparator.reverseOrder()))
        .limit(5);
```

```java
// CORRECT: count descending, then word ascending as a tie-breaker
freq.entrySet().stream()
        .sorted(Map.Entry.<String, Long>comparingByValue(Comparator.reverseOrder())
                .thenComparing(Map.Entry.comparingByKey()))
        .limit(5);
```

```text
BANK=2
CREDIT=2
NATIONAL=2
PAYMENT=2
SALARY=2
```

### How do you keep the ranking as a map?

If a downstream needs the ranked result as a map rather than a list, collect the sorted entries into a `LinkedHashMap`, which preserves insertion order and therefore the ranking.

```java
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Comparator;
import static java.util.stream.Collectors.toMap;

LinkedHashMap<String, Long> rankedMap = freq.entrySet().stream()
        .sorted(Map.Entry.<String, Long>comparingByValue(Comparator.reverseOrder())
                .thenComparing(Map.Entry.comparingByKey()))
        .collect(toMap(
                Map.Entry::getKey,
                Map.Entry::getValue,
                (a, b) -> a,          // merge function (keys are unique here)
                LinkedHashMap::new)); // preserve ranking order
```

### How do `comparingByValue` and `comparingByKey` compare?

| Helper | Sorts by | Natural order | Reverse order |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `Map.Entry.comparingByValue()` | entry value | ascending count | `comparingByValue(reverseOrder())` |
| `Map.Entry.comparingByKey()` | entry key | ascending key | `comparingByKey(reverseOrder())` |

```java
// By value ascending
Map.Entry.comparingByValue();
// By value descending
Map.Entry.comparingByValue(Comparator.reverseOrder());
// By key ascending (good as a tie-breaker)
Map.Entry.comparingByKey();
```

### What are common mistakes?

A frequent mistake is using `Collectors.summingInt(e -> 1)` where `counting()` is clearer, or mixing up `counting()` (a `Long`) with an `int`, which causes an unboxing surprise.

```java
// WRONG: counting returns Long, assigning to long is fine but to int needs a cast
Map<String, Long> freq = words.stream().collect(groupingBy(w -> w, counting()));
// int c = freq.get("SALARY"); // compile error: Long cannot convert to int
```

```java
// CORRECT: keep it as Long, or convert explicitly
long c = freq.get("SALARY");
int ci = freq.get("SALARY").intValue();
```

Another mistake is forgetting the explicit type witness on `comparingByValue`, which can trip type inference when chaining `thenComparing`.

```java
// Clearer with an explicit type witness when chaining
.sorted(Map.Entry.<String, Long>comparingByValue(Comparator.reverseOrder())
        .thenComparing(Map.Entry.comparingByKey()))
```

### When should you use this pattern and what are the follow-ups?

Use the frequency-map-then-rank pattern whenever you need a histogram and a top-N list, such as finding the most common keywords in transaction narrations to feed an auto-categorization feature. Interviewers often follow up by asking why a map cannot be sorted by value in place (it has no value order), how to break ties deterministically (`thenComparing` on the key), and how to keep the ranking as a map (`LinkedHashMap`). A further follow-up is the difference between `counting()` and `summingLong`, where `counting()` always adds one per element and `summingLong` adds a computed amount.
